---
import Chapter from './Chapter.astro';
import ClaimsGame from '../quest/ClaimsGame.astro';
import { boSVG, markSVG, TEAM_COLORS } from '../../lib/crewbo/bo.js';
import '../../lib/crewbo/bo.css';

const [cobalt, teal, , ember] = TEAM_COLORS;
const team = [
  { who: 'riya', agent: 'Claude Code', os: 'Linux', c: cobalt.base, state: 'working' },
  { who: 'dev', agent: 'Codex', os: 'macOS', c: teal.base, state: 'working' },
  { who: 'maya', agent: 'Antigravity', os: 'Windows', c: ember.base, state: 'working' },
  { who: 'you', agent: 'waiting your turn', os: '', c: '', state: 'offline' },
] as const;
// The three laptops in the solution diagram; x is the left edge on the panel's 100-unit grid.
const nodes = team.slice(0, 3).map((t, i) => ({ ...t, x: 4 + i * 33 }));
---

<Chapter n="01" kicker="Imagine" title="It’s 2 a.m. and four agents want the same file.">
  <Fragment slot="panel">
    <div class="pg" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr">
      <p class="label-sm" style="grid-column:1/-1;margin:0">02:14 · six hours to the demo</p>
      {
        team.map((t) => (
          <div class="card laptop">
            <div class="bo-slot" set:html={boSVG({ state: t.state, color: t.c || undefined, size: 100 })} />
            <div>
              <strong>{t.who}</strong>
              <span class="mono sub">{t.agent}</span>
              <span class="mono sub">{t.os || '\u00a0'}</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        ))
      }
      <div class="bubble" style="grid-column:1/-1;justify-self:center">“who just rewrote session.ts?”</div>
    </div>
  </Fragment>
  <p class="lede">
    Imagine a hackathon. Four of you, one repository, six hours left. Each of you has an AI coding agent that can
    write a feature in minutes.
  </p>
  <p>
    So who gets to drive? If one person drives, three people watch. If everyone drives, four agents start editing the
    same files at once, each one sure it is alone, and nobody finds out until the merge.
  </p>
</Chapter>

<Chapter n="02" kicker="The problem" title="Agents can’t see each other. Git only tells you at the end.">
  <Fragment slot="panel">
    <div class="pg">
      <div class="term">
<span class="m">$ git merge dev/rate-limit</span>
CONFLICT (content): src/auth/session.ts
<span class="r">&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt; HEAD</span>
export const session = createSession(&#123; ttl: 3600 &#125;)
<span class="r">=======</span>
export const session = newSession(config.auth)
<span class="r">&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; dev/rate-limit</span></div>
      <div class="tag">found 40 minutes of work too late</div>
    </div>
    <p class="pcap">illustration</p>
  </Fragment>
  <p>
    Every coding agent assumes it owns the repository. None of them knows a teammate’s agent is editing the next file
    over, and git reports the collision only when someone merges, after both people have built on top of it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Before writing any code I interviewed ten teams. Nine of them mixed agent vendors in the same room: Claude Code on
    one laptop, Codex on another. So the answer could not belong to one vendor or one operating system.
  </p>
</Chapter>

<Chapter n="03" kicker="The solution" title="Claims that every agent respects, wherever it runs.">
  <Fragment slot="panel">
    <svg class="wires" viewBox="0 0 100 75" aria-hidden="true">
      <defs>
        <marker id="cb-head" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse">
          <path d="M0 0L10 5L0 10z" fill="currentColor" />
        </marker>
      </defs>
      {nodes.map((n, i) => <line x1={n.x + 13} y1="31" x2={40 + i * 10} y2="20" marker-start="url(#cb-head)" marker-end="url(#cb-head)" />)}
      {nodes.map((n, i) => <line class="dash" x1={n.x + 13} y1="47" x2={35 + i * 15} y2="57" marker-end="url(#cb-head)" />)}
    </svg>
    <div class="card box" style="left:28cqw;top:5cqw;width:44cqw;height:15cqw">
      <div class="row"><span class="hub-mark" set:html={markSVG({ size: 100 })} /><strong>Sync server</strong></div>
      <span class="mono sub">claims, tasks, status · <span class="no">never file contents</span></span>
    </div>
    {
      nodes.map((n) => (
        <div class="card box node" style={`left:${n.x}cqw;top:31cqw;width:26cqw;height:16cqw`}>
          <div class="bo-slot sm" set:html={boSVG({ state: 'working', color: n.c, size: 100 })} />
          <div><strong>{n.who}</strong><span class="mono sub">{n.os} · daemon</span></div>
        </div>
      ))
    }
    <div class="card box git" style="left:20cqw;top:57cqw;width:60cqw;height:12cqw">
      <strong>Your git remote</strong>
      <span class="mono sub">code, checkpoints, merges</span>
    </div>
  </Fragment>
  <p>
    With Crewbo, a teammate claims a file or a folder for their agent. Every agent’s own hooks ask the local Crewbo
    daemon before each edit, and an edit to someone else’s file is refused with a message the agent can act on:
  </p>
  <p class="quote mono">
    src/auth/session.ts is claimed by riya’s agent until at least 11:52 (Crewbo). Work on other files, or ask them to
    hand it over: crewbo claim request src/auth/session.ts
  </p>
  <p>
    Claims are leases, so a crashed agent cannot hold a file for long. Each task gets its own branch, Crewbo
    checkpoints it when the agent stops, and it compares every pair of checkpoints with git so both people hear about
    a conflict before anyone merges.
  </p>
</Chapter>

<ClaimsGame />

<Chapter n="04" kicker="My role" title="I founded it, designed it, and set the rules it is built by.">
  <Fragment slot="panel">
    <div class="ledger">
      <div><span>10</span>team interviews, before any code</div>
      <div><span>1</span>go / no-go gate with written kill criteria</div>
      <div><span>15</span>architecture decision records</div>
      <div><span>5</span>milestones, each opened by my approval</div>
      <div><span>3</span>operating systems tested live</div>
      <div><span>109</span>pull requests reviewed and merged by me</div>
    </div>
  </Fragment>
  <p>
    I run Crewbo the way I want its users to run their agents: with rules, not trust. I did the research, wrote the
    product requirements and kill criteria, made each architecture decision, ran the checks on Windows and across
    two machines, and reviewed every pull request before it merged.
  </p>
  <p>
    The engineering runs on written rules: verify every external fact against its source, claim nothing that was not
    run, record every costly decision, and pass a gate before each milestone. Every change runs on Linux,
    macOS and Windows in CI before it merges.
  </p>
</Chapter>

<Chapter n="05" kicker="Under the hood" title="A decision in milliseconds, with no network on the path.">
  <Fragment slot="panel">
    <div class="pg">
      <div class="term">
<span class="m"># maya's agent wants to edit a file</span>
Edit src/auth/session.ts
 └ PreToolUse hook → crewbo hook claude-code
    └ daemon: local claim set
       held by riya, lease 11:52
<span class="r"> ✕ deny</span>  <span class="m">(the agent reads the reason)</span>

<span class="m"># same check for shell commands</span>
sed -i 's/ttl/maxAge/' src/auth/session.ts
 └ parsed write target: src/auth/session.ts
<span class="r"> ✕ deny</span></div>
    </div>
    <p class="pcap">simplified</p>
  </Fragment>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The daemon answers from its own copy of the room’s claims,</strong> so an agent never waits on the
      network. Starting the hook process took 2.3 ms at the median in the first spike.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Hooks enforce; MCP only informs.</strong> In the spike, just telling the agent a file was claimed stopped
      it from trying. The hook’s refusal is the backstop, and it holds even with permissions bypassed.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Shell commands are parsed for the files they write,</strong> with a POSIX parser and a PowerShell
      tokenizer: 81% and 87% of writes caught before they run. A hash check afterwards reports the rest.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Conflicts come from git itself:</strong> <code>git merge-tree</code> on every pair of task checkpoints,
      across machines. Tasks a lead sends are signed with Ed25519 and run only after the teammate types <code>yes</code>.
    </li>
  </ul>
</Chapter>

<Chapter n="06" kicker="The hard part" title="The guard that failed open, silently.">
  <Fragment slot="panel">
    <div class="pg">
      <div class="term">
<span class="m">PS&gt; # Windows 11, Codex, hook installed at:</span>
C:/dev/spikes test/bin/probe.exe
<span class="m">PS&gt; codex exec "append a line to blocked.txt"</span>
<span class="g">✓ updated blocked.txt</span>

<span class="m">hooks run:</span>   <span class="r">0</span>
<span class="m">errors shown:</span> <span class="r">0</span></div>
      <div class="tag">one space in a path, and the guard is gone</div>
    </div>
    <p class="pcap">reconstructed from the spike notes</p>
  </Fragment>
  <p>
    During the Windows spike, a claimed file was edited anyway. No error, no warning. The cause was one space: Codex
    runs hooks through a shell, and an unquoted hook path containing a space never runs at all. Nothing in any
    documentation says so; it only showed up because every feature is tested live on every operating system.
  </p>
  <p>
    A coordination tool that fails open is worse than none, because the team believes it is protected. So the
    installer now refuses paths with spaces, and <code>crewbo doctor</code> checks for hooks that never fire. The same
    habit caught two more: a byte-order mark on hook input that made the parser read nothing and allow the edit, and a
    keychain call that blocked for over a minute when no secret service was running.
  </p>
</Chapter>

<style>
  .card.laptop { flex-direction: row; gap: 2.4cqw; align-items: center; height: 100%; }
  .bo-slot { width: 11cqw; flex: none; }
  .bo-slot.sm { width: 7cqw; }
  .bo-slot :global(svg), .hub-mark :global(svg) { width: 100%; height: auto; display: block; }
  .wires { position: absolute; inset: 0; width: 100%; height: 100%; color: var(--ink); }
  .wires line { stroke: currentColor; stroke-width: 0.35; }
  .wires .dash { stroke-dasharray: 1.2 1; color: var(--muted); }
  .box { position: absolute; justify-content: center; }
  .card.node { flex-direction: row; align-items: center; gap: 1.6cqw; padding: 1.6cqw 2cqw; }
  .row { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 1.4cqw; }
  .hub-mark { width: 4.6cqw; display: block; }
  .git { border-style: dashed; align-items: center; text-align: center; }
  .box:not(.node):not(.git) { align-items: center; text-align: center; }
  .no { color: var(--accent); }
  .quote { border-left: 3px solid var(--accent); padding: 10px 14px; background: var(--surface); font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5; }
</style>
